# Problem Solving and Reasoning Part II. S = questions in slide order, R = connection questions.
S = [
 ([2], "How do reorganization and insight relate to problem representation?", "A person can initially organize the elements in a way that hides a useful relationship. Reorganizing the representation can make a solution available, sometimes with the sudden realization associated with insight."),
 ([2], "What is problem solving as search?", "It concerns the activity between posing a problem and reaching its solution, finding a route through possible states. Specifying the starting conditions and the goal does not identify the sequence that connects them."),
 ([2], "How can the statement of a problem affect its difficulty?", "The presentation influences the relationships a person represents. In the mutilated checkerboard, considering total area hides the constraint that considering the pairing of different-colored squares makes available."),
 ([3], "What is the Russian marriage problem?", "There are 32 bachelors and 32 unmarried women, and a matchmaker arranges 32 heterosexual marriages. Two bachelors die, leaving 62 survivors, 30 men and 32 women, and the question is whether 31 heterosexual marriages can be formed."),
 ([3], "Why is the answer to the Russian marriage problem no?", "The two groups no longer have equal numbers, so at most 30 marriages can be formed and two women are left without a partner. Dividing 62 by two gives 31 pairs by total size, but that does not satisfy the requirement about which kinds of person each pair must contain."),
 ([4], "How does the mutilated checkerboard have the same structure as the Russian marriage problem?", "A domino covers two adjacent squares of different colors, and removing two opposite corners removes two squares of the same color. The colors are then unequal even though 62 squares matches the area of 31 dominoes."),
 ([4], "Why can rearranging the dominoes not fix the imbalance?", "Every complete covering would have to pair each square of one color with a square of the other, and one color is more numerous, so some squares would remain unmatched however the dominoes move."),
 ([5], "What is analogical problem solving?", "It uses the solution to a similar problem to guide the solution to a new problem. The target problem is the one currently being solved, and a source problem shares relevant similarities with it."),
 ([5], "How does the Russian marriage problem map onto the mutilated checkerboard?", "Marriages correspond to dominoes, the two groups of people correspond to the two square colors, and removing two bachelors corresponds to removing two same-colored squares."),
 ([5], "What is analogical transfer?", "It is the transfer from the source problem to the target problem. The useful information is the relationship among the elements, not their identical appearance."),
 ([6], "What is Duncker’s radiation problem?", "A patient has a malignant tumor in the stomach that cannot be operated on, and a ray can destroy the tumor if sufficiently high intensity reaches it at once."),
 ([6], "Why does a single ray fail in Duncker’s radiation problem?", "A high-intensity ray would also destroy the healthy tissue it passes through, and a low-intensity ray would spare the tissue but not destroy the tumor."),
 ([6], "How is Duncker’s radiation problem solved, and how many people solve it without an analogy?", "Several low-intensity rays converge on the tumor from different directions at the same time, so the combined intensity at the tumor is sufficient. Only about 10 percent of participants reach this without the helpful analogy."),
 ([7], "What is the fortress problem?", "A rebel general has an army strong enough to capture a dictator’s fortress if the entire force attacks it, but mines on each road detonate when a large force travels together."),
 ([7], "How is the fortress problem solved?", "The general divides the army into small groups that approach along different roads, each below the danger threshold, and the groups arrive together so their combined force can attack."),
 ([8], "How do the fortress and radiation solutions correspond?", "The fortress corresponds to the tumor, the small groups correspond to low-intensity rays and the roads correspond to paths through surrounding tissue. Sending one large force down one road corresponds to one damaging high-intensity ray."),
 ([8], "Why is convergence required and not merely dividing the force?", "Timing matters because the separate contributions must combine at the target. Dividing the army or lowering every ray’s intensity without combining the effects leaves the target requirement unmet."),
 ([9], "What are noticing, mapping and applying in Gick and Holyoak’s analogy steps?", "Noticing is recognizing that a source problem may be relevant, mapping connects the elements and relationships of the source to the target, and applying uses the correspondences to generate a parallel solution. Noticing is crucial and can be the most difficult."),
 ([9], "What happened after participants read the fortress story in Gick and Holyoak’s study?", "In Gick and Holyoak’s study, about 30 percent solved the radiation problem, so having the story available did not guarantee spontaneous use of its solution."),
 ([9], "What happened when participants were told to think about the story in Gick and Holyoak’s study?", "In Gick and Holyoak’s study, success rose to about 75 percent, because the reminder made a relevant source available for mapping and application."),
 ([10], "What are surface features?", "They are the specific elements of a problem, such as a fortress, an army, a tumor or a ray. Two problems can use very different objects while sharing the relationship needed for a solution."),
 ([10], "How can surface features hide a shared relationship, and how can they help?", "A fortress and a tumor do not look alike, which can keep a person from noticing the shared need for a strong combined effect. Making surface features more similar can make the relationship easier to notice."),
 ([11], "What is the lightbulb problem?", "Ruth finds an expensive laboratory lightbulb with a broken filament in sealed glass, and must fuse the filament without opening the bulb. A high-intensity laser would break the fragile glass, and a low-intensity beam would not fuse the filament."),
 ([11], "How is the lightbulb problem solved?", "Several low-intensity laser beams are directed toward the filament from different directions at once, so the combined effect fuses the filament while each part of the glass receives only a low-intensity beam."),
 ([12], "How well does Duncker’s radiation problem work as a source for the lightbulb problem?", "About 81 percent of participants who knew the radiation problem solved the lightbulb problem, compared with about 10 percent in the control group."),
 ([12], "Why is a connection between the radiation problem and the lightbulb problem easier to notice than one between the radiation problem and the fortress?", "The tasks share relatively similar surface features, since both direct a beam toward an internal target, which makes a possible connection easier to notice."),
 ([13], "What are structural features?", "Structural features are the underlying principle that governs the solution. In the radiation and fragile-glass problems, a strong beam would damage the surroundings on its way to an internal target."),
 ([13], "Why can two lightbulb versions have the same surface features but different structure?", "The same bulb, filament and lasers keep surface features similar, but changing why a single laser fails changes the relationship to the radiation target."),
 ([14], "How does the fragile-glass version match the radiation problem?", "A high-intensity beam would break the surrounding glass just as it would damage healthy tissue, so the source’s reason for using convergence corresponds to the target’s."),
 ([14], "How many participants solved the radiation problem after the fragile-glass source?", "About 69 percent."),
 ([15], "What is the insufficient-intensity version?", "The laser only generates low-intensity beams that cannot fuse the filament, and several lasers combine to supply enough intensity. There is no surrounding-damage constraint."),
 ([15], "How did transfer differ between the insufficient-intensity and fragile-glass sources?", "About 33 percent solved the radiation problem after the insufficient-intensity source, compared with about 69 percent after the fragile-glass source, even though both use converging laser beams."),
 ([16], "What is analogical encoding?", "It is the process of comparing two problems and determining the similarities between them, which draws attention to relationships that appear in both."),
 ([16], "How does analogical encoding improve analogical problem solving?", "Comparing source stories directs reasoning toward the structural features, so a later target can be approached through the common relationship and not only through one familiar situation."),
 ([17], "What is the analogical paradox?", "It is the contrast between difficulty applying analogies in laboratory tasks and the routine use of analogies in real-world settings."),
 ([17], "How many analogies do scientists use in a laboratory meeting?", "About 3 to 15 analogies in a one-hour laboratory meeting."),
 ([17], "What is in vivo problem-solving research?", "It observes people solving problems in real-world settings, which keeps the ordinary context. It can be time-consuming and researchers cannot isolate and control variables as in a constrained experiment."),
 ([18], "What is an expert?", "An expert is a person recognized as extremely knowledgeable or skilled in a particular field after devoting a large amount of time to learning, practicing and applying that learning."),
 ([18], "How do experts perform compared with beginners?", "A chess expert can have spent roughly 10,000 to 20,000 hours studying and playing, and experts solve problems within their field faster and with a higher success rate than beginners."),
 ([19], "What did Chase and Simon (1973) do?", "They compared a chess master with a beginner who had fewer than 100 hours of experience. Each viewed a chessboard for five seconds and tried to reproduce where the pieces had been, with actual game positions or random arrangements."),
 ([19], "What did Chase and Simon (1973) find?", "The expert did much better for actual game positions, because long-term memory made meaningful arrangements available as chunks. The same chunks were not available for random arrangements."),
 ([20], "How does knowledge organization affect problem solving?", "It affects how information can be accessed, since knowing more is accompanied by differences in how relevant problems are grouped and related to the principles needed to solve them."),
 ([20], "What did Chi et al. (1982) do?", "They presented 24 physics problems to experts, who were physics professors, and novices, who were students with one semester of physics, and participants sorted them into groups by similarity."),
 ([20], "How did novices and experts group the physics problems in Chi et al. (1982)?", "Novices grouped by surface features such as the appearance of the objects, and experts grouped by structural features, including the underlying principles."),
 ([21], "How did the novice and the expert group problem 24 in Chi et al. (1982)?", "In Chi et al. (1982), the novice grouped problems 23 and 24 because both involve inclined planes. The expert grouped problems 21 and 24 through conservation of energy, even though problem 21 has a block and spring on a horizontal surface."),
 ([22], "How do experts spend their time on problems?", "Experts spend more time analyzing a problem before proceeding, which can pay off in solving the overall problem efficiently."),
 ([22], "Is the advantage of experts general?", "No. It is specific to their field, and experts are no better than novices on problems outside it."),
 ([22], "How can expertise limit flexibility?", "Expertise can make a person less open to new ways of looking at a problem, and some problems require rejecting usual procedures for less frequently used ones."),
 ([23], "What is creativity, and what is divergent thinking?", "Creativity includes innovative thinking, novel ideas and new connections between existing ideas. Divergent thinking is open-ended and allows a large number of potential solutions."),
 ([23], "What does creative problem solving involve beyond getting an idea?", "It usually includes a lengthy period of trial and error as possibilities are developed and tested."),
 ([23], "What are the four parts of creative problem solving?", "Problem generation, which includes problem finding and fact finding, problem formulation, which includes defining the problem and finding ideas, problem solving, which includes evaluation and planning, and solution implementation, which means taking action."),
 ([24], "Why are ideas necessary but not enough in creative problem solving?", "Without ideas the process stops, but producing an idea does not complete the evaluation, planning and action involved in carrying a solution out."),
 ([24], "How can too much knowledge hinder creative problem solving?", "Familiar approaches can constrain which possibilities are considered, so an accepted procedure can dominate the representation even when the problem requires a different relationship."),
 ([24], "What does flexible thinking include?", "Being willing to reject an accepted procedure when it does not fit the problem and reconsidering how knowledge is being used."),
]
R = [
 ("How do the Russian marriage problem and the mutilated checkerboard show analogical transfer?", "The marriage problem is the source and the checkerboard is the target. Marriages map to dominoes and the two groups to the two colors, so recognizing the one-of-each pairing transfers the solution.", [3,4,5]),
 ("Why do noticing, mapping and applying each matter for analogy?", "Noticing finds a relevant source, mapping connects its elements to the target and applying generates the parallel solution. Gick and Holyoak’s results show that success can fail at noticing even when the source is known.", [5,9]),
 ("How does the fortress problem show the same structure as Duncker’s radiation problem?", "Both require enough force at a central target while limiting what travels along each approach, which is solved by combining weak approaches into a strong effect.", [6,7,8]),
 ("How do the 30 and 75 percent results of Gick and Holyoak and the 81 percent lightbulb result together show what helps transfer?", "A reminder to think about the story raised success from about 30 to about 75 percent, so noticing matters. The radiation source gave about 81 percent on the lightbulb problem, so similar surface features also help.", [9,12]),
 ("How do surface features and structural features each affect transfer?", "Similar surface features make a connection easier to notice, as in radiation to lightbulb. Structural features, the constraints that make convergence useful, determine whether the solution applies.", [10,12,13]),
 ("How did transfer from the fragile-glass source and the insufficient-intensity source compare for the radiation problem?", "About 69 percent solved the radiation problem after the fragile-glass source, which shares the surrounding-damage constraint, and about 33 percent after the insufficient-intensity source, which gives a different reason for converging beams. The different rates accompany a difference in the underlying constraints.", [14,15]),
 ("How does analogical encoding address the problem shown by Gick and Holyoak?", "Comparing sources draws attention to shared structure, which helps people notice and map relationships that they do not spontaneously use.", [9,16]),
 ("How do the lab difficulty of analogies and everyday use of analogies fit together?", "That contrast is the analogical paradox, and in vivo research studies analogies in real settings where scientists use many in one meeting, at the cost of experimental control.", [17]),
 ("What do Chase and Simon (1973) and Chi et al. (1982) show that is the same about expertise?", "In both, expertise organizes knowledge by meaningful relationships, chunks of piece positions in chess and structural principles in physics.", [19,20,21]),
 ("How do Chase and Simon (1973) and Chi et al. (1982) differ in what they measure?", "Chase and Simon measured memory for chess positions, while Chi et al. measured how problems are grouped by surface or structural features.", [19,20]),
 ("Why does the chess advantage disappear for random arrangements in Chase and Simon (1973)?", "In Chase and Simon (1973), the expert’s advantage comes from familiar chunks, and randomizing the pieces removes the relationships that experience makes useful.", [19]),
 ("How are novices’ surface grouping in Chi et al. (1982) and failures of analogical transfer in Gick and Holyoak related?", "Both reflect attention to surface features, whether similar-looking objects or different-looking scenarios, and not to the structural principle that governs the solution.", [10,20,21]),
 ("How can the experts’ analysis time and field specificity be explained by knowledge organization?", "Experts’ organized knowledge helps within the field, so extra analysis pays off there, but outside the field the organization does not supply the relationships needed.", [20,22]),
 ("How do an expert’s accepted procedure and a familiar approach dominating the representation compare?", "Both involve a familiar approach dominating, an accepted procedure for experts and a familiar approach that constrains which possibilities are considered, which can block a better approach.", [22,24]),
 ("How are representation and insight connected to analogy?", "In an analogy the relationship among the elements of a source is used to recognize the relationship in the target, which can make a solution available, as reorganizing a representation can.", [2,5,8]),
 ("How does divergent thinking relate to the four parts of creative problem solving?", "Divergent thinking generates many potential solutions, while formulation includes finding ideas, and evaluation, planning and implementation are separate parts that carry an idea through.", [23,24]),
 ("Why do creative problem solving and analogy both depend on how knowledge is used?", "Knowledge helps when it is organized around structure and hurts when a familiar approach dominates, so flexible use and noticing are what matter.", [9,22,24]),
 ("How are problem generation and problem formulation different from problem solving?", "Generation includes finding the problem and facts, formulation defines the problem and finds ideas, and solving includes evaluation and planning, after which implementation takes action.", [23]),
 ("How does the representation of a problem as a pairing explain two different problems?", "The pairing of two groups explains why both 62 survivors and 62 squares cannot be covered in pairs, so one representation solves two surface-different problems.", [3,4,5]),
 ("How do surface features and structural features each matter when a known solution is used on a new problem?", "Similar surface features make a connection easier to notice, and the structural features, the constraints that make a solution useful, determine whether it applies. The fragile-glass source worked better than the insufficient-intensity source because its structure matched.", [10,13,14,15]),
]
